---INSTRUCTION---
Write a function `determinant` that takes a square matrix (a list of lists of numbers) and returns its determinant.

The matrix will be represented as a list of rows, where each row is a list of numbers (integers or floats). The function must handle:
- 1x1 matrices by returning the single element.
- 2x2 matrices by returning the product of the main diagonal minus the product of the anti-diagonal.
- n x n matrices (where n > 2) by expanding recursively along the first row: for each element in the first row, multiply it by (-1)^column_index and the determinant of the submatrix formed by removing that row and column, then sum all these products.

The function must work for both integer and floating-point matrices.

---TEST---
```elixir
defmodule SolutionTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  test "determinant of 1x1 matrix" do
    assert Solution.determinant([[1]]) == 1
  end

  test "determinant of 2x2 matrix" do
    assert Solution.determinant([[1, 2], [3, 4]]) == -2
  end

  test "determinant of 3x3 identity matrix" do
    assert Solution.determinant([[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]]) == 1
  end
end
```

---REFERENCE---
```elixir
defmodule Solution do
  def determinant([[a]]) do
    a
  end

  def determinant([[a, b], [c, d]]) do
    a * d - b * c
  end

  def determinant(matrix) do
    size = length(matrix)
    Enum.with_index(matrix)
    |> Enum.reduce(0, fn {row, i}, det ->
      cofactor = :math.pow(-1, i) |> round()
      element = Enum.at(row, 0)
      submatrix =
        matrix
        |> List.delete_at(i)
        |> Enum.map(fn row -> tl(row) end)
      det + cofactor * element * determinant(submatrix)
    end)
  end
end
```

